  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "guinea-senegambia-representative-fusions-and-scenes",
      "title": "Guinea & Senegambia — Representative Fusions and Scenes",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** West African Roots | **Region:** africa-guinea | **Tier:** 7\n**Prerequisites:** regions-africa-guinea-06-history\n**Tags:** africa, guinea, west-africa, representative, fusions"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "festivals\n\nGuinea & Senegambia belongs to the **West African Roots** curriculum. Mory Kanté\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **festivals** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-mali",
      "title": "Compared with Mali",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Mande Music** (`regions-africa-mali-07-representative-depth`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **festivals**.\n\n| Topic | Mali anchor | Guinea & Senegambia (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | Afropop, festivals… | festivals… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-africa-mali-07-representative-depth`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-07-representative-depth`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "festivals\n\nStudy **guinea & senegambia** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of Guinea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Guinea) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **West African Roots**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **kora, balafon, voice**. Ensemble layer: **djembe ensemble**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **Mande forms**. Dance context: **dance**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **djembe tradition**. Contemporary scenes: **festivals**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Guinea percussion repertoire**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- festivals\n- Lead instruments: kora, balafon, voice\n- Ensemble: djembe ensemble\n- Forms: Mande forms\n- Dance: dance\n- History: djembe tradition\n- Scenes: festivals\n- Repertoire: Guinea percussion repertoire\n- Compared with Mali: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
